<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oni is a small town in the Racha region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, on the upper Rioni river. It was a seat of the eristavis (dukes) of Racha and had a long-established Jewish community, whose synagogue of the late 19th century still stands. The town was badly damaged by the Racha earthquake of 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oni lies in a deep gorge on the left bank of the Rioni river, some 830 metres above sea level and 210 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. It belongs to the Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and is, historically and ethnographically, part of Racha, a highland province of western Georgia. It is also the administrative centre of the Oni district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Archaeology has found artefacts of Colchian culture, among them Colchian coins of the sixth to third centuries BCE. Oni is first chronicled in the fifteenth century, though a legend credits its founding to King Pharnajom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the second century BCE. It stood at a crossroads between the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, Kartli, Imereti and Lower Racha, and developed into a typical late medieval commercial town, contested between the kings of Imereti and the princes of Racha.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> absorbed the town in 1810, and in 1846 it became the administrative centre of the Racha Uyezd of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi-governorate">Kutaisi Governorate</a>. In the Soviet period it was joined with surrounding villages to form the Oni district. The earthquake of 29 April 1991 measured 7.0 on the moment magnitude scale, with a maximum intensity of IX on the MSK scale, the most powerful recorded in the Caucasus Mountains, and it did significant damage to the town's infrastructure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Oni Synagogue was built in the 1880s by a Polish architect, with Greek Jews from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> as the builders. It recalls a large Jewish community, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s third largest after those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, most of whom emigrated to Israel after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; a few families remain. Most of the population is Georgian and Orthodox Christian, and the largest minority is Ossetian. The town and its surroundings hold ruined medieval forts and Georgian Orthodox churches, and the Racha Regional Museum has about 14,000 objects in archaeology, ethnography, numismatics, natural history, fine art, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and printed books.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oni, Georgia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
